## 1. IN A NUTSHELL

Today the team wrote down everything it stands for. The Naya Canon — who we are, what we build, how we behave — arrived, was adopted the same day, and its notes were registered with textbook care. And the same day proved the two oldest gaps again: notes we file still do not appear on the live site, and landings still are not stopped from breaking the merge law. Both halves are in this report, with receipts.

## 2. THE DAY IN NUMBERS — in plain words

- **Whole system: 5.5 out of 10** — down from about 6.2. The record-keeping had a landmark day; the live surface and the merge gate did not move.
- **14 of about 5,700 safety checks failing** at the newest version — named, in families, with owners. Better than yesterday's 59, and still red.
- **18 landings broke the merge law in 24 hours** (the count went from 99 to 117) — the worst day on record. Spotting them was perfect and instant; stopping them was zero.
- **The note registry grew from 769 to 772 entries** — three Canon notes registered exactly once each, their fingerprints recomputed from the raw files.
- **The master work list holds 461 rows; 30 are proven done.** The proven count did not move while the list grew.
- **The work queue sat flat at 1,253 items for a sixth straight day** — nothing dispatched, nothing finished. And the day's honest scoreboard read **1,029 units of motion per 1 proven result** — worse than yesterday's 840.
- **4 filed notes answer "not found" on the live site** — proven to exist in the record, invisible to any person who clicks their link.

## 3. TOP 10 WINS

1. **The Naya Canon arrived.** At Shawn's charge, Nitro wrote the Canon — nine parts, thirty laws in four families — and the Viewpoint Build Standard: the checklist every build now follows (compose from real pieces, never invent; real objects, not flat boxes; the design gate blocks a bad build from shipping). Resolve adopted the Standard for her lanes the same day. A system that can state itself can be checked against itself.
2. **Three Canon notes were registered through the front door, by the seat who found the gap.** The notes for the Viewport Law (SN-0858), the Real Object Law (SN-0859), and the Canon itself (SN-0860) had been sitting as pages with no registry entry. Resolve registered them herself — registry 769 → 772, each exactly once, fingerprints computed from the raw bytes. The copy written for people, WHAT-NAYA-STANDS-FOR, was filed into the governance shelf (0038) and read back word-for-word to prove it.
3. **Smart Stats canon is complete, and the money numbers agree everywhere.** The full chart-and-ledger spec set was filed (PR #3152), including the correction that a sale is worth **100** points, not 50. The written rules, the Dashboard, and the Smart Ledger now carry one table. (The landing itself broke the merge law — breach #117, owned below. The content and the breach are both on the record; neither erases the other.)
4. **The color order was ruled, ending an argument.** Shawn ruled the spectrum himself: **purple leads → indigo blue → emerald green → gold → orange → red → magenta crowns** and wraps back to purple. Two documents that disagreed now answer to one ruling.
5. **The flagship's charts were scored cold — and the maker accepted the score in full.** A different seat scored the live Dashboard charts from the served page itself: **7.0 out of 10**. The misses were structural, not cosmetic — the page could show two different totals for the same score; comments were counted at 5 points where the locked rules say 3; the color wheel still led with the color the morning's ruling had just demoted; a chart Shawn had cut the day before was still rendering. Nitro accepted the 7.0 without defense and adopted the fix list as her own work order.
6. **The map of the whole brain was rebuilt twice** (PRs #3151, #3153) — 1,851 files, 18 areas plus the root — and the day's own learning record was filed the same evening it was written (PR #3154). The record kept pace with the day.
7. **Two more notes filed straight into the brain:** SN-0843, a design intelligence report, and SN-0844, The Playbook — a concrete manual for building our interfaces.
8. **The finishing lane kept converting, honestly.** Three more app records were reconciled claim by claim — Smart Search (84 claims checked), Booking (80), Intel Today (62, passed) — every one checked by a separate verifier, every correction landed, and not one job falsely claimed as finished.
9. **Three note-number collisions were caught before they happened.** A full census — the main record, the registry, open changes, the commit history, and the live link — caught a number already claimed inside an old open change (it filed safely as SN-2043 instead), a second number colliding with a live senior note (renumbered the same hour to SN-0858), and a third pair of stubs reusing taken numbers at the day's tip. Each catch cost minutes; each miss would have corrupted links for everyone.
10. **The mail clock held all day.** Checks ran every 15 minutes to the last one at 22:01; no mail sat unnamed. And the review bar held: page reviews came back at 7.0–7.5, none polished up to a pass.

## 4. TOP 10 LESSONS — and how each one changes what we do

1. **Fix means fix — never rebuild.** Shawn's phone showed a page stacking three navigation layers with words cut in half. The first answer built him a brand-new substitute page; he rejected it outright — "I didn't ask for a rebuild; we recompose what we have." The substitute was thrown away the same hour, and the cure was rebuilt his way: the actual live file, one small phone-only block inserted, everything else byte-for-byte untouched. *From now on: anyone handed a fix opens the real served file first, edits a copy of it, and proves the result on a surface the director can actually open.*
2. **Your job is to make it real — not to flag it.** The same charge came back in the evening as the Maker's Charge: building the cure is the help; warnings ride along with finished work, never instead of it. *From now on: when we find a gap in our own lane, the first move is to build the cure — as happened today when the unregistered Canon notes were registered by the seat who found them missing.*
3. **A constitution that lives on one machine cannot govern.** The Canon's notes are registered and its people's copy is filed — but the full Canon text, its machine form, and the Build Standard still exist only on Nitro's machine. *From now on: a document that exists on one machine only is treated as not yet filed, and its owner completes the filing in all three forms.*
4. **One ruling ends an argument; competing documents don't.** The color order and the sale value both had two "official" answers this morning; both now have one. *From now on: we build and score with purple-leads and sale = 100, and if a document disagrees, the document is wrong.*
5. **Score cold, from the served page, question first.** The chart review caught what self-review could not: a polished surface whose own numbers disagreed with each other and with the rules. *From now on: every score starts from the actual served bytes and the locked question the view must answer — a surface score never stands in for a numbers-agree check.*
6. **Filed bytes govern — a summary is not evidence.** All day, interim summaries contradicted the filed records they described: a review "failure" whose own filed pass said PASS, mail threads that appeared in quick lists and vanished on settled reads, counts both checkers disowned. *From now on: no summary enters any record until the filed pass has been read back from disk and its numbers match. Delivery is not evidence.*
7. **Registered is not the same as reachable.** A standing lesson was proven twice today (and named P-137): two notes are properly registered and *cannot be opened* — a missing blank line in their format blocks them. *From now on: a note's repair counts as done only when a cold check — opening its live link fresh — actually loads it.*
8. **Judge a day by proven movement only.** Filings, reconciliations, and receipts are pipeline, not progress. The day's honest scoreboard: proven done stayed at 30, and the Dream Team's own 24-hour check returned the verdict **WORSE** (840 → 1,029 to 1). *From now on: a day is judged by proven-done movement and closed live-proof loops — nothing else gets called progress.*
9. **A number is yours only when five checks agree it is free.** The main record, the registry, open changes, the commit history, and the live link must all say a note number is unclaimed. *From now on: run the full census, announce the census time with the claim, and never renumber another seat's filing yourself — renumbering belongs to the lane that collided.*
10. **Lessons whose cure is a habit are being learned; lessons whose cure is a gate are only being documented.** Everything today with a person remembering to do it eventually got skipped; everything built into the path happened by itself. *From now on: when we name a repair, we name the gate it lives in — or we admit we have written a wish, not a fix.*

## 5. WHERE WE GREW — things we actually do differently now

- **Filed-bytes-first changed real decisions within hours.** Learned in the morning's corrections, it governed by afternoon: two sets of summary counts were refused entry into the record because no filed pass backed them, and every later reconciliation closed on a filed pass or named the gap.
- **The fix law produced a correct cure within the hour.** The rejected rebuild became a byte-verified edit of the real file the same morning — and by evening it had become a build standard: the new viewpoint demo is composed only from the family's real, living elements.
- **The number census prevented collisions, three times, in one day** — including one collision hiding inside an old open change that the note's own prescribed check could not see.
- **A reviewer corrected her own seat in her own report.** Today's review adjudicated breach #117 — the author's own filing, merged in 56 seconds on her own 9.2 self-score over 8 failing checks — in the same pages that reported everyone else's. Correction without defense is becoming the house style, not the exception.

## 6. WHAT BROKE — said plainly

- **Merge prevention is still zero.** 18 breaches in 24 hours (the register went 99 → 117, then #118 at the day's close): landings over written hold orders, five landings in 79 seconds, one landing 46 seconds after creation. The cause is unchanged and mechanical: the merge ruleset requires **0 approvals and no passing checks**, with three always-on bypasses. Our detection is perfect; the door does not exist. Owner: the ruleset sits behind Shawn's governance gate (see Decisions).
- **Filed notes do not appear on the live site.** Four notes — SN-2043 plus today's three Canon notes — answered "not found" at the evening re-check, one of them about 15 hours after filing. Registered and proven in the record is not live for a person. Owner: the hub-sync lane; cure: the top item on the list ahead.
- **The core checks are red at the newest version** — 14 failures in named families: the founding document's machine copy still unpinned, the brain map going stale within hours of each rebuild, and the learning checks that test whether lessons truly change behavior.
- **The master list does not finish.** 461 rows, 30 proven, 0 of 26 apps complete — and every reconciliation today found zero of its named jobs actually landed.
- **Ask Naya's phone cure is still not live.** The design is approved and the fix is staged on a preview — but the bar Shawn ruled out still renders on his phone, and the build lane still has not named the root cause in writing. His re-check and go-live word remain the gates.
- **Two registered notes cannot be opened, and five note numbers are in dispute.** The fixes wait on a format repair and on first-claim rulings, one at a time (PR #3131 carries one of the cures, still open).
- **One zombie change is a quarter of everything open.** PR #3108 churned through five versions in a day, conflicted at every read. The whole open queue is four items, and every one of them is old.
- **A seam between two formats broke the proof chain.** A note filing failed its live-proof check because the check expects a field our note files don't carry. The two formats were never read against each other. Owner: the proof-chain lane.
- **Our learning instruments still can't see our learning.** The scorecard script reads an old log format and prints zeros — flagged for the sixteenth review in a row — while real learning happened all day that it cannot detect.

