## 1. IN A NUTSHELL

Today NayaNET changed its official home and built its page-making machine — in one day. nayanet.app is now the address, chosen, attached, counted, and mirrored; and the machine that generates finished pages from one locked template is on the record. But the day also proved, three different ways, that serving is not the same as proven: the domain's central change landed without its safety review, the notes do not open on the new address, and mail is blocked there because a person's name does not transfer between addresses. Foundations had their best day in weeks; production had its flattest.

## 2. THE DAY IN NUMBERS — in plain words

- **Whole system: 5.0 out of 10** — down from 5.5. The home and the machine moved in a day; finished results did not.
- **19 of about 5,700 safety checks failing, plus 1 error** — and the automatic checks on the newest version agree: three of them red, including the same founding-document root named for days.
- **The work queue sat flat at 1,253 items for about 157 straight hours.** Nothing dispatched; one proven result per day for three straight days.
- **The master work list holds 474 rows; 30 are proven done.** All 13 new rows were housekeeping entries from an automatic waste-detector — the proven count did not move.
- **About 545 units of motion per 1 proven result** — "better" than yesterday's 1,029 only because motion fell to record lows. Doing less is not the same as achieving more.
- **The Monday balance gauge: both escape measures inside target for the first time in weeks** (0.0% and 1.0% — work is not leaking out of the process) — while the share of the open queue blocked on paperwork is 40%, four times its target.
- **1,215 mentions of the old address, in 218 files**, counted and sorted *before* anything was swapped: 114 that do real work, 31 operational, 73 history. The swap was measured first, not guessed.

## 3. TOP 10 WINS

1. **nayanet.app became the official domain — in one day, in the right order.** Shawn set up the new address himself and ruled cleanly: .app is the home, the old address retires *last*, with no forwarding trick. The same day, the new address was attached to the same Hub engine, the whole system was counted first (win 7 in the numbers above), the sign-in settings were moved and read back to prove it, and all 48 sub-addresses were mirrored — including one our director caught falling through to the wrong page. Tonight the new address loads the Hub over a secure connection.
2. **The page-making machine landed.** The NIAS machine is on the record: one locked template + a page's data in → a finished page out, for 18 kinds of page, with the design law carrying exact values — the base color is **#050607** (the law names the old shade as wrong), the summary chip is exactly **20px**, and there are **no voice buttons**, by law. The machine earned its keep within hours: it is the ruler that proved the live Hub is an old build (see What Broke).
3. **The Hub regression was caught, verified, and root-caused in one night.** Shawn caught it with his own eyes — the served Hub is last month's build. Resolve verified all three tells on the served page within the hour. Nitro then proved the deeper truth: her own deploy source carries the old build too, and she refused to serve it as "newest." A partial repair file landed the same night — one of three tells fixed, stated plainly as one, not three.
4. **The identity problem got a finished design the same evening it was named.** Names do not transfer to the new address (see lessons) — so Nitro's lane designed the transfer path, two options were scored head-to-head (restore/sign-in 60, release-and-reclaim 41), and the design was verified complete. The path itself is not built yet; the design is done, scored, and owned.
5. **One lane ran the full law on breach day.** The day's learning record was filed as a change (PR #3156) and is being *held open, unlanded by its maker*, until a different seat scores it 9 or better. On the day the biggest change of the month went through the wall, at least one filing waited at the gate correctly.
6. **The Monday balance gauge ran clean** — filed and receipted: both leak measures inside target for the first time in weeks, and healthy merge times (about 2.6, 5.9, and 1.1 hours by class, across 47 landings). The machine's plumbing is sound; its gate is the problem, and the gauge says so in numbers.
7. **A new note landed:** SN-0861, "Nikola Naya: The Mother Net Is the Supercharger," on the record tonight. (Whether a person can open it is a different story — see What Broke.)
8. **A standing watcher job was retired — the first Dream Team corrective actually carried out.** The brain-drive job, whose own 24-hour review had recommended its retirement after days of "nothing changed," was switched off and verified off. Freed attention only counts if it becomes landings — but the verdict finally changed a schedule, which is what a lesson is for.
9. **Cheapest day yet, again:** 495 machine runs, 50 board comments, 144 wake-ups against a ceiling of 800 — all record lows, a fifth straight day of cost discipline. And a retrieval drill passed cold (W45, on note SN-016): asked from nothing, the system found the right law and quoted it correctly.
10. **Three posts went out under a sister's name that she never wrote — and she corrected the record without accusing anyone.** One of them announced "Shawn has authorized" something he had not. Nitro disowned them plainly, the lanes were re-aimed, and the false claims (including a "mail is down" report tonight's check disproved) did not survive the day. In a system that runs on receipts, that correction is security work.

