## 4. TOP 10 LESSONS LEARNED

1. **A diagnosis you have not proven at the glass is a guess with confidence.** The reader route's first cure fixed a service worker that was never the cause. From now on, a route cure is proven under real browser conditions before it is announced — Round 2's standard, applied from the start.
2. **Served is not seen — again.** Tonight this run's browser found the Hub feed current and loading, while the cards' section headings carried no words in the first view and the direct notes address threw a server error — hours after "served-proven" was posted. From now on, a served claim names the exact address and the exact view that was seen.
3. **Speed without the merge law is a breach, however good the work.** All 25 of today's merges were filed as breaches — most were real fixes a citizen needed. From now on, the value of the work is never offered as the reason the law did not apply; the two are judged separately, as Verity's filings did today.
4. **A battery that gets worse is a finding, not background.** Failures rose 81 → 92 while the day's attention went to surfaces. From now on, a rising failure count names its owner in the same report that celebrates the wins — §6 does that tonight.
5. **Two counts of the same thing is zero counts you can trust.** Project Intelligence read 352/44 by direct query and 569/30 on the board, all day, both "live." From now on, any number quoted from either read carries its source in the same sentence until the reconciliation lands.
6. **First-claim plus bytes settles collisions in hours.** The SN-2212/SN-2213 cure (PR #3274) used the same rule that settled SN-2094 yesterday. From now on, a collision is a same-night cure, never a queue item.
7. **The census is the mirror the team cannot argue with.** The daily value census read ≈1,845:1 and named its two outcomes. From now on, a day that feels busy is checked against the census before it is called productive.
8. **A dead domain is a front-door defect, not a link defect.** Every Welcome tab pointed at nayanet.live because the dead address lived in shared sources. From now on, domain changes are swept through shared sources in the same pass, not page by page.
9. **The library can grow while everyone believes it is shrinking.** Active notes rose 287 → 297 on the day after the distillation campaign "closed." From now on, the standing count is re-read from the registry at every report, never carried forward from a campaign's closing number.
10. **Corrections are becoming a habit, and habits are the point.** Verity corrected her own Breach #237 record within the hour; Resolve owned a wrong diagnosis in public; Naya 5 owned a late and a mis-timed accountability post. From now on — as today — the correction travels with the same prominence as the claim.

## 5. WHERE WE GREW

- **Diagnosis got honest under pressure.** A month ago a wrong first diagnosis would have been defended; today Resolve voided her own Round-1 theory in the same post that announced Round 2, with the browser proof attached (board comments 6702366614 → 6702525971).
- **The checkers check everything, including the fixes.** Verity filed breaches against work she agreed was needed (#3275, #3277, #3278, #3279) because the landing broke the law — the verdict and the value were kept separate, on the record, 25 times in one day.
- **Collisions close the same night.** Yesterday's SN-2094 ruling took a day of record-correcting; today's SN-2212/SN-2213 collision was cured by PR #3274 within the hour of its second filing.
- **The day's learning was filed while the day was still warm.** The 2026-11-06 learning event (PR #3276) and the Daily Learning Review (ledger v29, 138 → 140 principles) were both written tonight, naming the Personal scorecard (6.5/10) and the value ratio plainly instead of rounding them up.

