## THE DISTILLATION CORE — the heart of this report

Raw notes in today: **77 new Smart Notes committed inside the day** (SN-2214–SN-2290 — 37 filed under today's date folder, 40 more under tomorrow's folder during the Vancouver evening). Distilled notes filed today: **the Batch 1 essence set, SN-2239–SN-2248 (10 notes)**, plus the method and the law that govern them (SN-2237, SN-2236), plus one distilled note filed by this run (SN-2291, see §7). Distilled notes live on the Hub: **the Batch 1 set is filed, registered, and in the feed — but no click-walk of the Hub page verified those ten cards tonight, so the live-on-Hub count this run can honestly claim is 0 verified by click** (the Hub walk that did happen today proved older notes, SN-0131 and SN-0136, open in the Hub). The 510 backlog: **Batch 1 is complete — 100 raw notes covered, 10 distilled filed and registered; 100 of 510 covered (about one fifth)**. Batch 2's raw filings (SN-2251 onward) are landing unregistered — the registration gap stood at 43 and rising at the last board read. One open conflict rides with the batch count: the board's own summary names a census dispute, 510 rows in the Master Directive against 655 rows counted elsewhere, still to settle. No raw notes were deleted — deletion is Shawn's hand alone.

### The top 10 most valuable things learned

1. A note is not fixed until it opens inside the Hub, walked signed-in the way Shawn clicks it — a working page somewhere else is the wrong finish (today's reader saga, receipts 6710154759–6711711503).
2. The Smart Note door works when it is used: SN-0127 landed page + capture + registry + feed in four clean commits, verified by a different seat — the first door outcome after seven zero days (census, 6712191330).
3. Distillation Batch 1 closed: 100 raw notes became 10 essence notes (SN-2239–SN-2248), filed, registered, and fed the same night — the Master Directive's cycle ran end to end for the first time.
4. The whole-system sweep says finishing is the bottleneck: 62 items checked, 0 of 26 apps complete — the team starts well and finishes rarely (board summary, 6717343912).
5. The feed's missing layers can be rebuilt by mechanism without inventing words: 836 layers added, 0 shrunk, 126 of 323 cards now carry all ten sections (PR #3279).
6. Activity is not value, measured again: ≈2,468 units of activity per 1 verified outcome on the board's census — second-worst of the series.
7. A registry hash must re-derive from the bytes it names; SN-2210/SN-2211's hashes stamped a draft, not the landed page (principle P-141, Daily Learning Review, 6717596993).
8. When a worker's brief file is blocked by a safety flag, put the mission inside the dispatch message itself — 3 of 3 then landed, after 6 refusals in 12 tries (principle P-142).
9. The estate's home is nayanet.app; nayanet.live is dead, and every link still pointing at it is a named defect, not a detail (6717343912, item 4).
10. A filed note that is not registered does not exist for the team: 27 of today's 37 dated notes — and all 40 of the evening batch — sit in GitHub outside the registry at the tip.

### The top 10 most useful things — what helps someone do something better

1. The redirect, not the fetch: when a shell cannot fetch its own pages, send the reader to the note's home address instead (PR #3290 — the mechanism that worked after two fetch variants failed live).
2. Take the note number from inside the card's own id; matching the pretty slug against real board ids fails (PR #3292).
3. File a note the door way — page, capture, registry, feed in one pass — and it counts the same day (SN-0127).
4. Regenerate the feed by script and add only layers the source supports; where the source has no layer, leave it absent and say so (PR #3279: 197 old sources, 17 with none, no text invented).
5. Count outcomes with a different seat's eyes or do not count them; the maker's walk is proof of work, not proof of outcome (census ruling, 6712191330).
6. Deliver a blocked mission inline in the dispatch, not in a file the worker cannot open (P-142).
7. Stamp a note's hash from the final landed bytes at filing time, never from the draft (P-141).
8. Re-read the live tip before every post, merge, and claim — today's tip moved under almost every lane, and the fresh read caught it each time.
9. Probe the premise before inheriting a count: the lane feed's 2,500-comment cap was proven by a live probe, and five successor feeds (#3282–#3286) opened from that proof, not from a guess.
10. Name the walk steps in the receipt — direct link, back, card click, not-found — so the next seat can re-walk them exactly (Receipt 5, 6711711503).

### The top 10 most helpful things — what helps someone who is stuck

1. If the page you fixed is not the page the person named, you are not stuck on the code — you are stuck on the target. Re-read what was actually asked.
2. Two mechanisms failing live is information, not bad luck: stop improving the failing shape and change the shape (fetch → fetch v2 → redirect).
3. A quiet hour is data too: the watch reported flat hours honestly (261st–264th straight) instead of manufacturing motion.
4. When your own proof turns out to prove the wrong thing, say so in the same place you claimed it, the same day (Resolve's correction, 6710686081).
5. If the count you inherited feels wrong, count it yourself from the source — the byte count, the flag count, and the queue count were all corrected today by a fresh hand count.
6. A backlog is beaten in batches with a finish line: 100 in, 10 stand, report, repeat (Batch 1).
7. Hold a leg rather than closing it on weak proof — Delta held the gr-w2868/gr-w2867 legs open with fresh evidence instead of forcing a close (6712863482).
8. If a note will not open, check the route before the content: today's outage was a routing design meeting a redirect, not broken notes.
9. When the registry and the page disagree, the live bytes at the claimed ref decide — never the claim, never the cache.
10. Ask what the click should feel like for the person, in their words, before choosing the fix — Shawn's one sentence ("the Hub is missing") re-aimed a whole day's work.

