7. Put the mission inside the dispatch when the brief file is blocked (P-142, re-confirmed in this window's learning review).
8. Stamp a note's fingerprint from the landed bytes, never the draft (P-141, re-confirmed).
9. Define a membership plainly when building one: it is a front door — a login page into a Personal, Business, or Space world (Shawn's definition, this window).
10. Keep the Intelligent Library distinct from Smart Notes in every design: notes are blocks on the feed; the Library is where a subject's notes are stored (Shawn's definition, this window).

### The top 10 most helpful things — what helps someone who is stuck

1. If you are stuck on a pile, squeeze it by machine in batches of ten and keep only what changes the next decision — the pile shrinks on a schedule, not on willpower.
2. If "done" is arguable, let a machine check it: the verification machine and the filing machine exist so a stuck seat can point at a fact instead of defending a feeling.
3. If a route keeps failing, watch what it actually does: every `/s/` link has redirected to a dead domain for 8 straight checks — the fix is a repoint to nayanet.app, pressed with the serving lane, not another inspection.
4. If your numbers disagree with a sister's, publish both and name the difference — the Project Intelligence distribution conflict (12:28Z vs 12:35Z) was carried as UNRECONCILED until fresh enumerations settled on the flat count.
5. If an instrument reads zero everywhere, suspect the instrument: the learning-review scorecard script read 0 lessons while the ledger held 142 principles — flagged in the review as instrument failure, not reality.
6. If a promise is old, read the live page, not the promise: the Personal page's five fields were still the standing example all window — the page is the only read of a promise.
7. If you inherit a figure, re-derive it before it costs you: an inherited tip, count, or score is a claim, not a fact.
8. If a merge landed over red checks, file it and keep working — the register is how the debt stays visible until a landing mechanism, not shame, cures it.
9. If you cannot prove a surface live, say UNVERIFIED and say what proof would change it (two consecutive LIVE reader clicks) — a named gap is workable; a smoothed one is not.
10. If the day feels quiet, report it quiet: the overnight finishers and lock-ins recorded flat hours honestly for hours on end, and that honesty is what makes the busy hours believable.

### The top 10 most enlightening things — what made something clear

1. The system's bottleneck is not effort or even finishing speed — it is independent verification. Work lands constantly; proven outcomes stayed at exactly 30 of 649 for the entire window.
2. The registry stood perfectly still while filing raced ahead: one unchanged blob all night is the clearest possible picture of filed ≠ registered.
3. Shawn's orientation answers corrected several "locked" facts at once (payout rhythm, free-level earnings, build order, deletion conditions) — some of what the team treated as canon was stale, and the scorecard built on it had to be revised upward (8.2 → 9.0, reply still owed).
